WEBVTT


00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:05.780
Hello, welcome back to our course, and today we are going to continue talking about time series.

00:00:05.916 --> 00:00:10.916
So a time series is composed of these two elements, so the time periods.

00:00:11.135 --> 00:00:22.135
And the value of our variable of interest. For example, in the last video, we saw that that particular value could be, for example, the sales of a shampoo.

00:00:22.609 --> 00:00:31.609
And then, time series can be decomposed into an increasing or decreasing trend. This is a systematic component.

00:00:32.072 --> 00:00:42.072
Into a repeating short-term cycle. This is also a systematic component, and into a random variation, or a noise. This is an unsystematic component.

00:00:41.516 --> 00:00:49.516
Then we have different trend functions. We could try to fit the values of my XT, the observed values.

00:00:49.831 --> 00:00:55.831
We could have a geometric mean trend function, so basically it means that I try to model

00:00:55.349 --> 00:01:02.349
My values using the first element to times the rate parameter to the power of T minus 1. 

00:01:03.103 --> 00:01:09.103
We could also have a linear trend model, so basically here I would like to just fit a linear regression.

00:01:08.485 --> 00:01:17.485
We could also have an exponential trend, so we try to explain the sales using a model which uses observed values.

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:24.190
But now, my model is exponent over parameter times the exponent of another parameter to the power of t.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:29.430
But here, bear in mind that we could apply on the left-hand side and right-hand side

00:01:35.043 --> 00:01:37.043
logarithm, and then this boils down to fitting a linear regression to log of my observed values.

00:01:36.463 --> 00:01:43.463
And we also can have a moving average trend function. So here, this moving average trend function, you can see that this is a sum over j's,

00:01:48.658 --> 00:01:51.658
and then we have a certain weight. And then we basically weigh our jth plus 1 elements

00:01:51.426 --> 00:01:58.426
from the particular period to the jth element. Alright, we could also have seasonality with sub-periods

00:01:59.144 --> 00:02:10.144
small p, from 1 to capital P, so capital P can be 4. When we have a seasonal seasonality measured quarterly, or, for example,

00:02:15.296 --> 00:02:19.296
that capital P could be 12, where we have monthly seasonality. And then

00:02:17.522 --> 00:02:24.522
if T falls in subperiod P, then we could have this additive effect

00:02:24.878 --> 00:02:29.878
of seasonality, so basically we have this value from the trend

00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:38.053
and the value from the seasonality effect. And then we could also have a multiplicative effect.

00:02:44.099 --> 00:02:47.099
So basically, the value from the trend, it's multiplied by seasonality. And then here you can see that in both cases, 

00:02:46.923 --> 00:02:55.923
We compute this seasonality effects slightly differently, so in the additive case, we have a mean of the observed value minus the trend.

00:02:56.317 --> 00:03:01.317
And then in multiplicative term, you have a mean of the observed value over

00:03:09.436 --> 00:03:11.436
The computed value from the trend. And here, just to show you a little bit better, what does this

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:18.890
term where we have both trend and seasonality looks like. So, for example, if we look at this value of

00:03:25.493 --> 00:03:30.493
[capital] of hat x_tp for t equals 2, and for S equals 2, so basically this is this multiplicative

00:03:30.985 --> 00:03:35.985
seasonality, so we have the trend predicted using the trend

00:03:35.968 --> 00:03:41.968
modeling, and then we have seasonality predicted using the seasonality, so basically seasonality here

00:03:45.922 --> 00:03:48.922
was computed using the mean over the observed value over the value for the trend.

00:03:48.783 --> 00:03:55.783
And then, we could evaluate our model using this R squared, so basically we try to understand

00:04:01.036 --> 00:04:03.036
how much of the variation in my target parameter was explained by my model.

00:04:03.016 --> 00:04:08.016
So basically, here, in my model, we have both trend and seasonality.

00:04:07.737 --> 00:04:17.737
Alright, so let us now look at some data analysis. So here, first, let us look at the time series with a geometric mean model. So basically, first,

00:04:20.172 --> 00:04:22.172
we have to compute rates. 

00:04:21.364 --> 00:04:29.364
So, our N refers to the number of rows, the number of observation here in my XT value.

00:04:29.822 --> 00:04:34.822
And then, on the right, we have seen the formula for the right, so the right

00:04:35.007 --> 00:04:47.007
was completed as this last value of the sales over the first value to the power of what? 1 over N minus 1. So this is our rate value, our rate value is close to 1.

00:04:50.724 --> 00:04:53.724
And then you can see that now I could use that geometric mean trend model

00:04:53.435 --> 00:05:03.435
To try to predict the number of sales, so basically here we have the first value times the rate parameter to the power of 0 to N-1,

00:05:03.668 --> 00:05:10.668
so basically we estimate this geometric model, and then you can see that I could plot it. So basically, in this plot

00:05:14.602 --> 00:05:17.602
we have our observed sales. And then we have this trend

00:05:24.269 --> 00:05:27.269
plotted using geometric mean model. Alright, we could also use a linear trend model, so here, for the linear trend,

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:34.190
first, what am I trying to do? I'm trying to coerce this time series to numeric values, so here you can see that I'm trying to do that.

00:05:40.647 --> 00:05:44.647
And then I'm creating a data frame, DF. So the data frame, which has, two variables, variable T,

00:05:47.124 --> 00:05:49.124
from 1 to the length of my vector.

00:05:48.583 --> 00:05:56.583
And then, the second parameter is, basically the sales here. So the sales are now,

00:05:57.041 --> 00:06:02.041
coerced from the time series. So basically, these are just the sales levels,

00:06:02.879 --> 00:06:07.879
and here I can fit this linear trend model, so a linear trend model here.

00:06:18.052 --> 00:06:21.052
I'm regressing T, so the time periods, on the sales. And then, here you can see the call of this function, we can see the residuals, they do not appear to be normal at all.

00:06:30.665 --> 00:06:34.665
And then we can see that our estimates, the intercept was fitted, and the coefficient of T was fitted, and both of them are significantly different from 0.

00:06:34.696 --> 00:06:40.696
Now we could also look at the exponential trend model. So here, a reminder that,

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:52.110
we could basically, measure this trend using the exponential trend model, meaning that I have an exponent on a coefficient times the exponent on another coefficient to the power of t.

00:06:51.909 --> 00:06:58.909
But if I apply log on the left and right-hand side, then actually, I could once again go back and model

00:07:02.559 --> 00:07:05.559
log of my sales using a linear model. And indeed, here you can see that

00:07:06.037 --> 00:07:11.037
what do we have? We have a linear model applied to the log of my sales.

00:07:11.287 --> 00:07:23.287
And then I regressed T on that log of the sales, and my data is just from the data frame I have just created. And in this call of this function, we have also coefficients.

00:07:24.014 --> 00:07:30.014
And then we could also compute R squared, so we saw the formula for R squared, so how much of the

00:07:29.624 --> 00:07:39.624
variation, so we can see variance of data here, is explained by our model, and then we can see that almost 80%.

00:07:42.734 --> 00:07:44.734
This is not a bad result. And then, let us now plot

00:07:44.705 --> 00:07:50.705
our trends. So here you can see that I created a plotting window where 

00:07:51.022 --> 00:07:56.022
I have T on the x-axis, I have sales on the y-axis, and then

00:08:01.601 --> 00:08:04.601
in red, I have a linear trend, you can see. And then, in blue, I'm plotting the exponential trend, but here you can see that

00:08:04.764 --> 00:08:14.764
I need to go back to the exponent, because I applied here the log, and now I need to go back, so basically I apply exponent to the fitted value of my

00:08:14.845 --> 00:08:20.845
linear model, because before I used a linear model for just for the fitting.

00:08:21.266 --> 00:08:26.266
And then you can see that, indeed, this exponential trend looks pretty good in this plot.

00:08:34.767 --> 00:08:38.767
Now, let me pass to another time series. So here, I'm seeing the number of births per month in New York City from 

00:08:37.444 --> 00:08:43.444
January 1946 to December 1958, and once again, here, I would like to import the data first.

00:08:54.749 --> 00:08:57.749
The data was stored under this address. And, here, I'm transforming this XT object to time series object. I'm selecting the starting value, I'm selecting the frequency,

00:08:57.578 --> 00:09:00.578
and then you can see that if I plot the data,

00:09:00.824 --> 00:09:07.824
we have, both trend, and we have seasonality, and then I plotted the data by birth per month

00:09:14.857 --> 00:09:16.857
over the time periods. And then here, if we would like to decompose that time series object, first we could use the

00:09:16.789 --> 00:09:25.789
decomposition for moving average, and additive seasonality. So here, if I apply this function decompose to a time series object

00:09:25.789 --> 00:09:32.789
then, I have this object DT, and then if I look at the attributes of DT, what do I have? I have time series,

00:09:32.926 --> 00:09:38.926
and then you can see that we extracted this additive seasonal trends here.

00:09:43.074 --> 00:09:46.074
And now, we could also, have another decomposition, we could have a trend,

00:09:45.326 --> 00:09:54.326
and then we could have a moving average with multiplicative seasonality. So here, I need to just select the type to multiplicative.

00:09:55.105 --> 00:10:00.105
And now, let us just plot all the time series components, so here in this plot, what can we see?

00:10:07.685 --> 00:10:10.685
We could see our observed values. So, here, it's just enough to apply a plot function to a DT data frame.

00:10:14.367 --> 00:10:18.367
And then, here, we also have a trend decomposed, we have a seasonal effect

00:10:18.752 --> 00:10:26.752
and then we have this random, unsystematic component plotted. So we see, you can see very clearly how this

00:10:32.659 --> 00:10:35.659
time series can be decomposed into these three parts thanks to the application of the function decompose to t. OK, that's it for today, thank you!

